# Database migrations

Nothing currently catches a model changed without a matching migration —
CI only runs `alembic upgrade head` (applies existing migrations), never an
autogenerate diff against the models. A model edit that isn't followed by a
migration will pass CI and fail later, at runtime, in whatever environment
notices the missing column first.

- **Every change to a model in `app/models/*.py` needs a migration in the
  same PR.** Generate it with `make migrate-create` (prompts for a message,
  runs `alembic revision --autogenerate`), don't hand-write one unless
  autogenerate can't express the change.
- **Read the generated migration before committing it.** Autogenerate is
  unreliable for PostGIS/geometry columns (`app/models/geo.py`) and for
  `server_default`/trigger-backed columns (see the `updated_at` trigger
  documented in `docs/architecture.md`) — it can miss them entirely or
  generate a spurious drop/recreate. Diff it against the model change you
  actually intended.
- **Never edit an already-merged migration in `migrations/versions/`.** If a
  past migration was wrong, add a new one that corrects it — editing history
  breaks anyone who already ran `alembic upgrade head` against the old
  version.
- **Test locally with `make db-reset`** (drops, recreates, runs every
  migration from scratch) before relying on `make migrate` (upgrades an
  existing db) alone — the latter won't catch a migration that only works
  because your local db already happened to be in the right prior state.
